{"id":"karabiner-98k","slug":"karabiner-98k","name":"Karabiner 98k","aliases":["K98k","Kar98","Kar98k","KAR 98","Kar 98K","Mauser 98k","Mauser K98","Gevär m/40","Gewehr 98 k","Spanish M43","Karabiner 98","Scoped Kar98k","Carabiner 98k","Mauser Kar98k","Mauser Kar 98k","Karabineer 98k","Karabiner 98 Kurz","Mauser Karabiner 98k","Mauser Model 1898 rifle","Karabiner 98k (bolt-action rifle)"],"kind":"firearm","category":"historical","subcategory":"German bolt-action rifle","description":"The Karabiner 98 kurz, often abbreviated Karabiner 98k, Kar98k or K98k and also sometimes incorrectly referred to as a K98, is a bolt-action rifle chambered for the 7.92×57mm Mauser cartridge. It was adopted on 21 June 1935 as the standard service rifle by the German Wehrmacht. It was one of the final developments in the long line of Mauser military rifles.","history":"In February 1934, the Heereswaffenamt (Army Weapons Agency) ordered the adoption of a new military rifle. The Karabiner 98k was derived from earlier rifles, namely the Mauser Standardmodell of 1924 and the Karabiner 98b, which in turn had both been developed from the Gewehr 98. Since the Karabiner 98k rifle was shorter than the earlier Karabiner 98b (the 98b was a carbine in name only, as it was in reality a version of the Gewehr 98 long rifle designated a carbine to adhere to the Treaty of Versailles; the 98b was additionally fitted with a tangent rear sight rather than the more claustrophobic \"Lange\" ramp sight), the new rifle was given the designation Karabiner 98 kurz, meaning \"Carbine 98 Short\". Just like its predecessor, the rifle was noted for its reliability, safety and an effective range of up to 500 metres (550 yd) with iron sights  and 1,000 metres (1,090 yd) with an 8× telescopic sight.\nThe desire for adopting new shorter barreled rifles and the introduction of the Karabiner 98k, featuring a 600 mm (23.62 in) long barrel, were reasons for changing the standard German service ball rifle cartridge. The 1903 pattern 7.92×57mm Mauser S Patrone produced excessive muzzle flash when fired from arms that did not have a long barrel like the Gewehr 98. It was found that the s.S. Patrone, originally designed for long range machine gun use, produced less muzzle flash out of rifles that had a shorter barrel and also provided better accuracy. Because of this, the S Patrone was phased out in 1933 and the s.S. Patrone became the standard German service ball cartridge in the 1930s.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"7.92×57mm Mauser"},{"label":"Action","value":"Bolt-action"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"760 m/s","numeric":760,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"5-round stripper clip, internal magazine"},{"label":"Sights","value":"Iron sights or telescopic sight."}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"3.7–4.1 kg"},{"label":"Length","value":"1110 mm","numeric":1110,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"600 mm","numeric":600,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"500 m with iron sights","numeric":500,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Effective Range","value":"800 or with telescopic sight"},{"label":"Maximum Range","value":"4700 m with s.S. Patrone","numeric":4700,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designed","value":"1934"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Mauser (augmented by several other makers)"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1934–1945"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"14,600,000+"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"55 (1935)"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"DE EUR current equivalent"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Nazi Germany"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1934–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"7.92×57mm Mauser","Action":"Bolt-action","Muzzle Velocity":"760 m/s","Feed System":"5-round stripper clip, internal magazine","Sights":"Iron sights or telescopic sight.","Weight":"3.7–4.1 kg","Length":"1110 mm","Barrel Length":"600 mm","Effective Range":"500 m with iron sights","Maximum Range":"4700 m with s.S. Patrone","Designed":"1934","Manufacturer":"Mauser (augmented by several other makers)","Produced":"1934–1945","Number Built":"14,600,000+","Unit Cost":"55 (1935)","Origin":"Nazi Germany","In Service":"1934–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Kar_98K_AM021488_noBG.png","width":2200,"height":1000,"license":"See Wikimedia Commons"},"thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Kar_98K_AM021488_noBG.png/330px-Kar_98K_AM021488_noBG.png"},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Bagnet_wyj%C4%99ty_z_pochwy.JPG","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagnet_wyj%C4%99ty_z_pochwy.JPG","width":3312,"height":1632,"caption":"Bagnet wyjęty z pochwy","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","attribution":"Kominek1993"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/2june_2007_187.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2june_2007_187.jpg","width":2592,"height":1944,"caption":"Soldiers of the MOD Germany \"Bundeswehr Guard Battalion\" on a parade in Rome (Italy).","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","attribution":"Utente:Jollyroger"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Circular_error_probable_-_example.png","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circular_error_probable_-_example.png","width":1898,"height":1888,"caption":"example to Circular error probable with 20 points.","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","attribution":"Ariel196"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-584-2159-20%2C_Frankreich%2C_Soldat_mit_Gewehr_in_Stellung.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wik